/**
 * Computed signatures of the `para` module (C++ operators, modules/para).
 *
 * `unordered_foreach` and `unordered_reduce` take the element type of their array argument as the
 * type of the function they call; the `DynamicFuncTypeResolver` of modules/para is the one source
 * of those signatures, so they are not declared in stdlib/para.cml. Their documentation and `#op`
 * tags live here, and scripts/stdlib-reference.mjs reads this file (the module's `computed:` entry)
 * together with stdlib/para.cml.
 *
 * For reference only: these declarations are not compiled. They are checked against the operators
 * the module registers, and docs/stdlib-reference.md is generated from them.
 */

/// Calls f for every element; the calls have no order among them.
/// @effect what f does
/// @example xs->unordered_foreach<work>
#op<':mark/unordered_foreach_arr'>
with <f: (item: T) => void>
export func unordered_foreach(xs: T[]): void

/// Combines the elements with f starting from initial; the order in which elements are
/// combined is not fixed, so f should be associative and commutative.
/// @effect what f does
/// @example xs->unordered_reduce<add, 0>
#op<':mark/unordered_reduce_arr'>
with <f: (acc: T, item: T) => T, initial: T>
export func unordered_reduce(xs: T[]): T